  "story": {
    "headline": "A 39-second doublet that moved the ground half a metre",
    "what": "Two earthquakes 39 seconds apart (M7.2 then M7.5) ruptured the Caribbean-South American plate boundary near Yumare on 24 June 2026. NISAR imaged the ground 11 days before and about one day after, measuring up to 32 cm of line-of-sight motion across 6717 km\u00b2.",
    "physics": "InSAR compares the phase of the radar wave between two passes. Each full colour cycle in the fringe image is half a wavelength of range change, about 12 cm at L-band. Counting fringes outward from the fault converts directly into centimetres of ground movement. The sign flip across the fault trace is the signature of strike-slip faulting: one side moves toward the satellite, the other away.",
    "drivers": "The Caribbean plate slides eastward past South America at roughly 20 mm/yr. Strain accumulated on this boundary released in a shallow 10 km rupture.",
    "caveats": "Line-of-sight motion mixes vertical and horizontal movement; separating them needs the descending track. Median coherence is 0.37 over vegetated terrain, so 39% of pixels are masked. Later pairs include post-seismic motion and any residual atmospheric phase."
  },
